619 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1430 0 R
/E (Iterative Development: Because it breaks development into smaller cycles, it allows for regular feedback and course correction, making it more flexible than the traditional Waterfall model.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
620 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1431 0 R
/E (High Quality: By integrating testing and quality verification into every iteration \(rather than saving it for the end\), the final product tends to be more robust and bug-free.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
621 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1432 0 R
/E (Component Reuse: The focus on architecture and modular components makes it easier to reuse parts of the code for future projects, saving time and money in the long run.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
622 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1433 0 R
/E (Comprehensive Documentation: RUP provides a clear "paper trail" for every stage of development. This is vital for large organizations that need to maintain systems over many years or meet strict regulatory requirements.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
623 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 398 0 R
/T (ADVANTAGES)
/E (ADVANTAGES)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [5]
